      "html_url": "https://github.com/amartya1523",
      "followers_url": "https://api.github.com/users/amartya1523/followers",
      "following_url": "https://api.github.com/users/amartya1523/following%7B/other_user%7D",
      "gists_url": "https://api.github.com/users/amartya1523/gists%7B/gist_id%7D",
      "starred_url": "https://api.github.com/users/amartya1523/starred%7B/owner%7D%7B/repo%7D",
      "subscriptions_url": "https://api.github.com/users/amartya1523/subscriptions",
      "organizations_url": "https://api.github.com/users/amartya1523/orgs",
      "repos_url": "https://api.github.com/users/amartya1523/repos",
      "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/amartya1523/events%7B/privacy%7D",
      "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/amartya1523/received_events",
      "type": "User",
      "site_admin": false,
      "name": null,
      "patch_url": null
    },
    "body": "This addresses the \"Reorganize self-test\" item in #1835.\n\nThe SHA256 check used by `secp256k1_selftest` and `secp256k1_context_set_sha256_compression` only exercises the hash module, so the first commit moves it next to `secp256k1_sha256_smoke_test` and renames it to `secp256k1_sha256_selftest`. No functional change.\n\nAfter that, `selftest.h` only held a one-line wrapper, so the second commit removes the file and has `secp256k1_selftest` call the hash self-test directly. A comment there explains why it always uses the built-in implementation: the API function is meant to catch a broken build of the library (e.g. wrong endianness), and a compression override is already checked when it is set on a context.\n\nI kept the move and the removal as separate commits so the first one is easy to review as a pure move.",
